{:edb/db-before-t 1605 :edb/db-after-t 1606 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534918 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:57:33.809Z" 13194139534918 true] [17592186100688 1100 "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120$, which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)$?" 13194139534918 false] [17592186100688 1100 "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139534918 true] [17592186100688 1145 "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005$, and the carrying capacity is $a=120$. Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P)\n$$" 13194139534918 false] [17592186100688 1145 "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=120.$ Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P).\n$$" 13194139534918 true] [17592186100690 1100 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" 13194139534918 true] [17592186100690 1100 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" 13194139534918 false] [17592186100690 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7$, and the carrying capacity is $a=8$." 13194139534918 false] [17592186100690 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8.$" 13194139534918 true] [17592186100857 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319751 13194139534918 true] [17592186100857 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319758 13194139534918 true] [17592186100857 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319765 13194139534918 true] [17592186100857 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319786 13194139534918 true] [17592186100857 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319793 13194139534918 true] [17592186100858 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319730 13194139534918 true] [17592186100858 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319737 13194139534918 true] [17592186100858 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319744 13194139534918 true] [17592186100858 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319772 13194139534918 true] [17592186100858 1020 #edb/ref 17592186319779 13194139534918 true] [17592186319730 1017 #edb/ref 17592186319731 13194139534918 true] [17592186319730 1089 "q-119264" 13194139534918 true] [17592186319730 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534918 true] [17592186319730 1100 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139534918 true] [17592186319730 1113 #uuid "1fd05112-8082-5953-be66-8ced5469a502" 13194139534918 true] [17592186319730 1145 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50.$" 13194139534918 true] [17592186319731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319732 13194139534918 true] [17592186319731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319733 13194139534918 true] [17592186319731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319734 13194139534918 true] [17592186319731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319735 13194139534918 true] [17592186319731 1000 #edb/ref 17592186319736 13194139534918 true] [17592186319731 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534918 tr